# Gitleaks rule/config files intentionally contain literal secret-shaped
# strings (PEM/PGP key headers, vendor obfuscated-secret regexes) so gitleaks
# itself can match them. semgrep's p/secrets ruleset flags the same literal
# text as a hardcoded secret. .gitleaks.toml already carries an [allowlist]
# for gitleaks with this rationale; this is the semgrep-side equivalent.
.gitleaks-vendor.toml
.gitleaks.toml
